window.docContents[193].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-hypercube-and-the-hamming-cube":{"contents":"When we first introduced the hypercube we gave two other constructions. One of these\nwas the Hamming cube hypercubeHamming: the graph on Fin n → Bool in which two\nvertices are adjacent iff their Hamming distance, computed by Mathlib's hammingDist\nfor Pi-types, is one. Having identified the graph distance in Q[n] with the Hamming\ndistance (edist_eq_hammingDist), we are now in a position to prove that this\nconstruction agrees with the one we adopted, i.e. Q[n] ≃g hypercubeHamming n.\n\nFirst we need to identify the two vertex types, i.e. give an equiv\nHypercubeVert n ≃ (Fin n → Bool). We build this by recursion on n. The base case\nholds since both types have exactly one element. In the inductive step we send\n(v, b) to the function whose first n coordinates are those of v and whose last\ncoordinate is b. Mathlib's Fin.snoc, which appends an element at the end of a\ntuple, does exactly this (and Fin.snocEquiv is Fin.snoc as an equiv).\n\n#check @Fin.snoc      -- append an element at the end of an n-tuple\n#check Fin.snocEquiv  -- Fin.snoc as an equiv\n\n\nThe equiv between the two vertex types of the hypercube, HypercubeVert n and\nFin n → Bool, sending (v, b) to Fin.snoc (equivPi n v) b, so b is the last\ncoordinate./-- The equiv between the two vertex types of the hypercube, HypercubeVert n and\nFin n → Bool, sending (v, b) to Fin.snoc (equivPi n v) b, so b is the last\ncoordinate. -/\ndef HypercubeVert.equivPi : (n : ℕ) → HypercubeVert n ≃ (Fin n → Bool)\n  | 0 => Equiv.ofUnique Unit _\n  | n + 1 => (equivPi n).prodCongr (Equiv.refl _) |>.trans <|\n      (Equiv.prodComm _ _).trans <| Fin.snocEquiv fun _ => _\n\n\nUnfolding the equiv one step is definitionally Fin.snoc, and marking this as\n@[simp] lets simp compute with equivPi recursively.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.equivPi_succ_apply {n : ℕ} (v : HypercubeVert n) (b : Bool) :\n    equivPi (n + 1) (v, b) = Fin.snoc (v.equivPi n) b := rfl\n\n\nTo transfer distances along the equiv we need to know how Mathlib's hammingDist\ninteracts with Fin.snoc: appending a coordinate to each of two vertices increases\ntheir Hamming distance by one exactly when the appended coordinates differ. (Note how\nthis mirrors the recursion in HypercubeVert.hammingDist.) To prove this we write\nthe Hamming distance as a sum of indicators, using Finset.card_filter, and split\noff the last coordinate with Fin.sum_univ_castSucc.\n\nlemma hammingDist_snoc {n : ℕ} {β : Type*} [DecidableEq β] (x y : Fin n → β)\n    (a b : β) :\n    hammingDist (Fin.snoc x a : Fin (n + 1) → β) (Fin.snoc y b) =\n      hammingDist x y + if a = b then 0 else 1 := by\n  simp [hammingDist, Finset.card_filter, Fin.sum_univ_castSucc]\n\n\nThe Hamming distance of HypercubeVert n vertices agrees with the Hamming distance\nof the corresponding functions Fin n → Bool, by induction on n.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.hammingDist_equivPi {n : ℕ} (u v : HypercubeVert n) :\n    _root_.hammingDist (u.equivPi n) (v.equivPi n) = u.hammingDist v := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => rfl\n  | succ n ih =>\n    obtain ⟨u, b⟩ := u\n    obtain ⟨v, c⟩ := v\n    simp [hammingDist_snoc, ih]\n\n\nAdjacency in the Hamming cube is Hamming distance one, by definition.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming_adj {n : ℕ} {x y : Fin n → Bool} :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).Adj x y ↔ hammingDist x y = 1 := Iff.rfl\n\n\nWe can now give the graph isomorphism: the underlying equiv is\nHypercubeVert.equivPi, and it respects adjacency since both adjacency relations say\nexactly \"Hamming distance one\".\n\nThe hypercube Q[n] (the box product construction) is isomorphic to the Hamming\ncube construction hypercubeHamming n on Fin n → Bool./-- The hypercube Q[n] (the box product construction) is isomorphic to the Hamming\ncube construction hypercubeHamming n on Fin n → Bool. -/\ndef hypercubeIsoHamming (n : ℕ) : Q[n] ≃g hypercubeHamming n where\n  toEquiv := HypercubeVert.equivPi n\n  map_rel_iff' := by\n    simp [hypercube_adj_iff_hammingDist_eq_one]\n\n\nThis isomorphism is a bridge between the two constructions: results proved for\nQ[n] by induction transport to hypercubeHamming, while statements that need\nthe coordinates of a vertex, such as identifying the automorphism group of the\nhypercube, are more naturally proved for hypercubeHamming and transported back.\n\nTransporting edist_eq_hammingDist along hypercubeIsoHamming shows that the graph\ndistance in the Hamming cube is exactly the Hamming distance.\n\nlemma edist_hypercubeHamming {n : ℕ} (x y : Fin n → Bool) :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).edist x y = hammingDist x y := by\n  obtain ⟨u, rfl⟩ := (HypercubeVert.equivPi n).surjective x\n  obtain ⟨v, rfl⟩ := (HypercubeVert.equivPi n).surjective y\n  exact ((hypercubeIsoHamming n).edist_eq u v).trans (by simp [edist_eq_hammingDist])\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory","header":"12.5. The Hypercube and the Hamming Cube","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-hypercube-and-the-hamming-cube"},"/Connectivity/#section-connectivity":{"contents":"In Mathlib connectedness is defined in two steps: a graph is preconnected if there\nis a walk between every pair of vertices, and a graph is connected if it is\npreconnected and nonempty.\n\nRecall from Reachability that, for a single pair of vertices\nu v : V, G.Reachable u v means that there is a walk from u to v.\n\n#source Preconnected  -- def Preconnected : Prop := ∀ u v : V, G.Reachable u v\n#source Connected     -- structure Connected : Prop where\n                      --   protected preconnected : G.Preconnected\n                      --   protected [nonempty : Nonempty V]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity","header":"10.1. Connectivity","id":"/Connectivity/#section-connectivity"},"/Theorems/#section-hall-s-marriage-theorem":{"contents":"Let G be bipartite with parts p₁ and p₂ (in Lean G.IsBipartiteWith p₁ p₂).\nHall's condition says that every finite set s ⊆ p₁ has at least |s| neighbours.\nHall's theorem says that this condition implies that G has a matching saturating\np₁, that is, one whose vertices include all of p₁. (The converse is easy: the\npartners of the vertices in s are distinct neighbours of s.)\n\nA bipartite graph with a subset S and its open neighbourhood N(S)\n\n#check exists_isMatching_of_forall_ncard_le\n\n\nNotice that V need not be finite: G only needs to be locally finite. Hall's\ncondition is stated with Set.ncard, which is 0 on infinite sets, so it holds\ntrivially for infinite s and only finite sets need to be checked.\n\n#check Set.Infinite.ncard -- s.Infinite → s.ncard = 0\n\n\nexample {s : Set V} (hs : s.Infinite) :\n    s.ncard ≤ (⋃ x ∈ s, G.neighborSet x).ncard := by\n  simp [hs.ncard]\n\n\nFor a perfect matching, Mathlib asks for Hall's condition for every set s of\nvertices, which may contain vertices from both sides.\n\n#check exists_isPerfectMatching_of_forall_ncard_le\n\n\nBoth theorems come from the statement about systems of distinct representatives: a\nfamily of finite sets t : ι → Finset α has an injective choice function iff every\nunion of k of the sets has at least k elements. Mathlib states it for any index\ntype ι. For infinite ι it follows from the finite case by compactness, as for the\nDe Bruijn–Erdős theorem (colorable_of_forall_finite_subgraph_colorable): each\nfinite set of indices has at least one injective choice and only finitely many, and\nnonempty_sections_of_finite_inverse_system makes the choices agree.\n\n#check Finset.all_card_le_biUnion_card_iff_exists_injective\n\n\nMathlib applies it to the finsets G.neighborFinset x with x ∈ p₁, and it is to\nform these that it assumes [G.LocallyFinite]. For a perfect matching it applies it\nto all of V, which is why the condition is stated for all sets of vertices. This\ngives injections p₁ → p₂ and p₂ → p₁ along edges. If V is finite then\n|p₁| = |p₂|, and either one is already a perfect matching. In general Mathlib\ncombines them using a relational form of the Schröder–Bernstein theorem\n(Function.Embedding.schroeder_bernstein_of_rel), which yields a bijection p₁ → p₂\nalong edges.\n\n#check exists_bijective_of_forall_ncard_le\n\n\nMathlib's proofs build the matching from the chosen neighbours by hand, but the same\nconstruction is available as a lemma: a bijection f : s ≃ t between disjoint sets,\nwith each v adjacent to f v, gives a matching with vertex set s ∪ t.\n\n#check Subgraph.IsMatching.exists_of_disjoint_sets_of_equiv\n\n\nIn a bipartite graph, an injective choice of neighbours f for the vertices of\nX ⊆ p₁ is such a bijection onto its range, since the range lies in p₂, which is\ndisjoint from X. So the edges x — f x form a matching covering X.\n\nlemma exists_isMatching_of_injective {p₁ p₂ X : Set V} (h₁ : G.IsBipartiteWith p₁ p₂)\n    (hX : X ⊆ p₁) (f : X → V) (hf : Function.Injective f)\n    (hadj : ∀ x : X, G.Adj x (f x)) :\n    ∃ M : Subgraph G, X ⊆ M.verts ∧ M.IsMatching := by\n  have hd : Disjoint X (Set.range f) := h₁.disjoint.mono hX\n    (Set.range_subset_iff.mpr fun x =>\n      h₁.mem_of_mem_adj (hX x.2) (hadj x))\n  obtain ⟨M, hverts, hM⟩ :=\n    Subgraph.IsMatching.exists_of_disjoint_sets_of_equiv\n      hd (Equiv.ofInjective f hf) hadj\n  exact ⟨M, hverts ▸ Set.subset_union_left, hM⟩\n\n\nLocal finiteness cannot be dropped from the classical theorem. Let a be adjacent to\nc₁, c₂, … and let each bᵢ be adjacent only to cᵢ. Every finite set of the\nvertices a, b₁, b₂, … has at least as many neighbours as elements, but no matching\ncovers them all: whichever cᵢ is matched to a, the vertex bᵢ is left unmatched.\n\nIn Lean, though, this graph fails Hall's condition for {a}: a has infinitely many\nneighbours, so the ncard of its neighbourhood is 0.\n\nexample {a : V} (ha : (G.neighborSet a).Infinite) :\n    ¬ ({a} : Set V).ncard ≤ (⋃ x ∈ ({a} : Set V), G.neighborSet x).ncard := by\n  simp [ha.ncard]\n\n\nSo Lean's form of Hall's condition already forces every vertex of p₁ to have a\nfinite neighbourhood. Applying the theorem on systems of distinct representatives to\nthese neighbourhoods instead of the finsets G.neighborFinset x, we can drop\n[G.LocallyFinite]. The vertices of p₂ may then have infinite degree: for\nexample, let p₁ = {b₁, b₂, …} and p₂ = {c₁, c₂, …} ∪ {z}, with bᵢ — cᵢ and z\nadjacent to every bᵢ.\n\nOne-sided Hall without [G.LocallyFinite]./-- One-sided Hall without [G.LocallyFinite]. -/\ntheorem exists_isMatching_of_forall_ncard_le' {p₁ p₂ : Set V}\n    (h₁ : G.IsBipartiteWith p₁ p₂)\n    (h₂ : ∀ s ⊆ p₁, s.ncard ≤ (⋃ x ∈ s, G.neighborSet x).ncard) :\n    ∃ M : Subgraph G, p₁ ⊆ M.verts ∧ M.IsMatching := by\n  classical\n  have hfin (x : p₁) : (G.neighborSet x).Finite := by\n    apply Set.finite_of_ncard_pos\n    simpa [Nat.succ_le_iff] using h₂ {x.1} (by simp)\n  obtain ⟨f, hf₁, hf₂⟩ := Finset.all_card_le_biUnion_card_iff_exists_injective\n      (fun x : p₁ ↦ (hfin x).toFinset) |>.mp fun s ↦ by\n    have := h₂ (s.image Subtype.val) (by simp)\n    rw [Set.ncard_coe_finset,\n      Finset.card_image_of_injective _ Subtype.val_injective] at this\n    simpa [← Set.ncard_coe_finset] using this\n  exact exists_isMatching_of_injective h₁ subset_rfl f hf₁ fun x : p₁ ↦ by\n    simpa using hf₂ x\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Matchings","header":"13.1.1. Hall's Marriage Theorem","id":"/Theorems/#section-hall-s-marriage-theorem"}});